Setting. In the 1986 'Analysis situs (deuxième mouture)' notes Grothendieck axiomatizes a 'tame' or combinatorial topology of 'figures'. The ingredients visible on this page and the previous one are:
- figures F with refinements F' ≪ F;
- restrictions F'|X to the pieces X ∈ F̃ of F;
- 'lieux' (loci), and an atlas M whose axioms are numbered At 9, At 9_Σ, At 10, At 11, At 11', At 12.

Page 79 records the dependency diagram between these axioms:
- At 9_Σ + At 10 is equivalent to At 9_Σ + At 12;
- At 12 ⇒ At 10 and At 12 ⇒ At 11';
- At 11 is 'gluing of refinements in F = sup F_i' for arbitrary index sets I;
- At 11' is the same for finite I;
- At 11 ⇔ At 11 bis + At 11'.
At 11 is the only axiom involving an infinite sup, so Grothendieck wants to replace it by something more natural.

This page states At 11 bis. Let F be a figure and (F'_i)_{i∈I} a filtered increasing family of refinements of F. Suppose that for every piece X ∈ F̃ the restricted family (F'_i|X) has a sup F_X. Then F' = Sup F'_i exists. In words: a filtered sup of refinements exists globally as soon as it exists locally on each piece.

The NB, read with p.79, says:
- At 11 bis follows from At 11;
- conversely At 11 bis together with At 11' gives back At 11.
This is the classical order-theoretic decomposition 'arbitrary sups = finite sups + directed sups', made local over the pieces X. He calls this the pleasant ('sympathique') reformulation, of the 'local finiteness' type. It turns the axioms into conditions on the existence of filtered sups, i.e. compatibility with limits.

The axioms then reduce to two systems:
- (I) At 9_Σ, At 10, At 11 bis. At 12 disappears, since At 9_Σ + At 10 ⇔ At 9_Σ + At 12.
- (II) At 9, At 11 bis, At 12. At 10 disappears, since At 12 ⇒ At 10.
In both systems At 11 and At 11' are absorbed: At 11' follows from At 9_Σ + At 10 in (I) and from At 12 in (II).

The drawback of (I) is that At 9_Σ depends on choosing a class Σ of index ordered sets. System (II) loses only the 'universal compatibility with limits' At 9_Σ. Grothendieck plans to recover it when passing to the saturation of the atlas, by putting At 9' = At 9_{Σ̄} (Σ̄ = all ordered sets) into the definition of a saturated atlas.

Purpose: housekeeping, minimizing and making the axioms of the atlas of figures more natural before the next page turns to subdivisions.
